  "raw_answer": "C1|Women with acute myocardial infarction often present with atypical symptoms such as abdominal pain, dyspnoea, nausea, back and neck pain, indigestion, palpitations, and unexplained fatigue rather than the classic chest pain seen in men|Passage 2|\nC2|Women are less likely to report their symptoms, which contributes to delayed or underdiagnosis of myocardial infarction|Passage 1|\nC3|Physicians may misdiagnose coronary heart disease symptoms in women as gastrointestinal or mental health conditions, contributing to delayed diagnosis|Passage 3|\nC4|Women are less frequently subjected to diagnostic tests such as exercise ECG and coronary angiography during the initial assessment of angina, which can lead to later diagnosis of myocardial infarction|Passage 4|",
